# 2026-03-25 19:18:57.496023
+This is a better plan than mine in several important ways. Honest comparison:
+
+  Where PLAN-cd wins:
+
+  1. Encrypted staging files. Using RAF directly as the staging file for advanced writes is the right
+  call. My plan keeps staging plaintext and only encrypts at upload time — that's simpler but means
+  plaintext sits on disk unnecessarily, and you pay a full encrypt-copy at flush time. PLAN-cd's
+  approach of replacing pwrite/pread with raf.write()/raf.read() on the staging file is more work but
+  eliminates that copy and keeps local disk ciphertext.
+  2. Metadata marking. This is a real gap in my plan. PLAN-cd uses content_type on BatchOp::AddFile
+  (application/vnd.hf-mount+enc; scheme=aegis-128x2-raf; chunk_size=65536) to tag encrypted files.
+  Without this, a mount can't distinguish encrypted from plaintext remote files — my plan just silently
+   assumes everything is encrypted when the flag is on, which breaks mixed-content scenarios and makes
+  it impossible to mount an encrypted repo without the key to even list files.
+  3. Forcing advanced writes. Instead of implementing an EncryptedStreamingWriter that buffers to a
+  local RAF and then re-uploads (my approach), PLAN-cd just forces advanced_writes = true when
+  encryption is on. This is pragmatic — the streaming path is append-only and doesn't map well to RAF.
+  One fewer abstraction to build and test.
+  4. Read path simplicity. Both plans materialize ciphertext to a local file and decrypt. But PLAN-cd
+  is more explicit about the flow: download → RAF open → decrypt to plaintext cache → serve as
+  OpenFile::Local. My plan wraps this in an EncryptedXetOps adapter which adds indirection without real
+   benefit since VirtualFs still needs to know about encryption for the OpenFile variant handling.
+
+  Where my plan (PLAN-cl) has a point:
+
+  - The adapter pattern keeps VirtualFs changes minimal, but this comes at the cost of correctness —
+  VirtualFs does need to know about encryption for the staging file I/O, size reporting, and open-file
+  variant selection. Trying to hide it behind XetOps is a leaky abstraction.
+
+  Bottom line: PLAN-cd is the one to implement. The main changes are more invasive (touching
+  write()/read() in VirtualFs to use RAF for encrypted staging) but they're the correct integration
+  points. I'd only add one thing from my plan: the EncryptedXetOps idea could still be useful for the
+  upload_files path in flush_batch, but it's not essential.

