# 2026-03-26 13:11:48.469146
+The plan is well-researched — line references are accurate and the claims about existing behavior check out against the
+  code. Here's my feedback:
+
+  Overall assessment
+
+  This is a high-quality, minimal-surface-area optimization plan. The key insight is sound: list_tree already proves the same
+   file identity fields that HEAD checks, so the per-file HEADs within the TTL window are genuinely redundant work. The
+  safety analysis is thorough and the invariants are clearly stated.
+
+  Substantive feedback
+
+  1. Step 5 should not be optional — it's load-bearing for practical value
+
+  This is the biggest issue. ensure_children_loaded() short-circuits when children_loaded == true, meaning it only actually
+  fetches (and thus stamps last_revalidated) on the initial load or after an invalidation+reload cycle. Without step 5:
+
+  - Directory loads, last_revalidated is stamped
+  - TTL passes, parent is now "stale"
+  - Lookups fall back to per-file HEADs
+  - But children_loaded is still true, so ensure_children_loaded won't re-fetch
+  - Parent freshness never refreshes — you're back to per-file HEADs permanently until the next poll invalidation
+
+  For long-lived mounts (the common case), the optimization would only help during the first TTL window after each directory
+  load. That's a much smaller win than the plan implies in the "Expected outcome" section. Promoting step 5 to required makes
+   the optimization actually deliver on its promise of "repeated lookups... should usually avoid per-file HEADs entirely."
+
+  2. Explicitly address the ensure_children_loaded short-circuit path
+
+  The plan references ensure_children_loaded but doesn't mention its early-return behavior when children_loaded == true. This
+   matters because it determines whether repeated readdir + lookup sequences refresh the timestamp. The plan should
+  explicitly state: "This stamp is set only when the listing is actually fetched, not on the short-circuit path when
+  children_loaded is already true."
+
+  3. Race window between parent read and child read under the lock
+
+  The plan says to compute parent_fresh while reading parent_entry in the fast path. Since lookup() already holds a read lock
+   on the inode table when it inspects both parent and child, this is fine — but worth explicitly stating that both reads
+  happen under the same lock acquisition, so there's no TOCTOU between checking parent freshness and reading child attrs.
+
+  4. Dual semantics on last_revalidated — pragmatic but worth a type-level guard
+
+  Reusing the field is pragmatic and I agree it's the right call for minimality. But the risk section's mitigation ("document
+   it clearly, keep reads guarded by kind == Directory") is a bit soft. A stronger low-cost option: add a
+  #[cfg(debug_assertions)] assertion in revalidate_file() that the inode is a file, and in the new parent-freshness check
+  that the inode is a directory. This catches accidental cross-use in tests without runtime cost.
+
+  5. Consider InodeKind::Symlink
+
+  The fast path gates on InodeKind::File. Symlinks pointing to files in the same directory have the same "already proven by
+  parent listing" property. Not critical for v1, but worth a sentence in the plan about whether this is an intentional
+  exclusion or a future opportunity.
+
+  6. Test coverage gap: the TTL boundary
+
+  The proposed tests cover fresh-parent-skips-HEAD and stale-parent-falls-back, but there's no test for the exact boundary —
+  i.e., lookup at metadata_ttl - epsilon succeeds from cache, lookup at metadata_ttl + epsilon triggers HEAD. This is worth
+  adding since off-by-one in TTL comparisons (< vs <=) can silently degrade the optimization.
+
+  Minor nits
+
+  - The plan references src/virtual_fs/poll.rs:217 for poll invalidation, but the actual invalidate_children call is at line
+  221. (Small discrepancy, not material.)
+  - "Stamp directory freshness on successful ensure_children_loaded()" — worth noting this should happen after the listing is
+   applied, not before, to avoid stamping freshness for a partially-applied listing on error.
+
+  Summary
+
+  The plan is sound and ready to implement with one key change: make step 5 non-optional. Without poll-based freshness
+  refresh, the optimization degrades to a one-shot benefit per directory load, which undermines the stated goal. Everything
+  else is solid — the safety invariants are well-reasoned, the implementation order is correct, and the test plan covers the
+  important cases.

