# 2026-03-26 13:41:52.744321
+It is a regression, in a narrow but real way.
+
+  Before this change, once metadata_ttl expired on a file's own last_revalidated, every lookup() fired a per-file HEAD — even
+   if the parent directory was still loaded. That HEAD could detect a remote change made by another device between poll
+  cycles.
+
+  After this change, if the parent directory was recently confirmed fresh (by initial load or by poll), lookup skips the HEAD
+   for the entire parent TTL window. So the only way to detect another device's modification to an existing file is via poll.
+
+  Concrete scenario:
+  1. Poll runs, confirms directory, stamps parent freshness
+  2. Another device modifies file.txt on the Hub
+  3. Within metadata_ttl, device B does lookup("file.txt")
+  4. Old behavior: file's last_revalidated is stale → HEAD fires → detects the change
+  5. New behavior: parent is fresh → HEAD suppressed → stale data served
+
+  The staleness window is still bounded by metadata_ttl, but the detection mechanism changed. Before, a stale file TTL
+  triggered an immediate HEAD on next access. Now, a fresh parent TTL masks it until either the parent TTL expires or the
+  next poll runs.
+
+  In practice the impact is small — both windows are bounded by the same metadata_ttl — but "same TTL budget" isn't strictly
+  true. The parent timestamp can be refreshed by poll independently of the file timestamp, so a file that would have expired
+  its own TTL and gotten a HEAD can now be kept "fresh" indefinitely by repeated poll confirmations, even though its content
+  changed remotely between two polls.
+
+  Want me to fix this? The cleanest approach would be to only let parent freshness suppress HEAD when the file has never been
+   individually revalidated (i.e., last_revalidated was set by insert(), not by a prior revalidate_file() call), preserving
+  the per-file HEAD cadence for files that have already entered the revalidation cycle.

